  <h2>Why the Seoul Metro is Your Best Friend</h2>
  <p>The Seoul Metro is world-class — clean, punctual, air-conditioned, and impossibly cheap. With 23 lines and over 700 stations, it reaches virtually every corner of the city. A single ride costs ₩1,400. For context, that's about $1 USD. Taxis start at ₩4,800 for the same distance.</p>

  <h2>Essential Lines for Tourists</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Line 2 (Green — Circle)</strong> — The most important line. Covers Hongdae, Sinchon, City Hall, Euljiro, Gangnam, COEX. Circular route means no wrong direction.</li>
    <li><strong>Line 4 (Blue)</strong> — Seoul Station, Myeongdong, Dongdaemun. Tourist central.</li>
    <li><strong>Line 3 (Orange)</strong> — Anguk (Gyeongbokgung Palace), Insadong, Express Bus Terminal.</li>
    <li><strong>Line 6 (Brown)</strong> — Itaewon, Mapo, World Cup Stadium.</li>
    <li><strong>Line 9 (Gold)</strong> — Express service: Gimpo Airport ↔ Gangnam in 30 min.</li>
    <li><strong>AREX (Airport Railroad)</strong> — Incheon Airport ↔ Seoul Station. Direct express: 43 min / ₩11,000. All-stop: 66 min / ₩9,500.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="tip-box">
    💡 <strong>Download Naver Maps</strong> before you land. It has real-time subway, bus, and walking directions in English. Better than Google Maps for Korea.
  </div>

  <h2>How to Pay</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>T-money card</strong> (best) — Rechargeable smart card. Buy at any convenience store. Cheapest fares + free transfers.</li>
    <li><strong>Single journey ticket</strong> — Buy at ticket machines. ₩100 more per ride + ₩500 deposit returned at exit.</li>
    <li><strong>Contactless credit card</strong> — Tap-to-pay works on most turnstiles (Visa/Mastercard). No transfer discounts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fares</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Up to 10km: <strong>₩1,400</strong> (T-money) / ₩1,500 (single ticket)</li>
    <li>10–40km: +₩100 per additional 5km</li>
    <li>40km+: +₩100 per additional 10km</li>
    <li>Free transfers (subway↔bus) within 30 min with T-money</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Best Navigation Apps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Naver Maps</strong> — #1 recommendation. Real-time, English, multi-modal</li>
    <li><strong>Kakao Maps</strong> — Great for restaurants and walking routes</li>
    <li><strong>Subway Korea</strong> — Simple offline subway map for quick reference</li>
    <li><strong>Google Maps</strong> — Works but less detailed than Naver in Korea</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Operating Hours</h2>
  <p>Most lines run approximately <strong>05:30 AM – 00:30 AM</strong> (varies by line and station). Last trains from busy stations like Hongdae or Gangnam can be around midnight. Check Naver Maps for the exact last train from your location.</p>
  <p><strong>Late-night options:</strong> Night buses (Owl Bus, N-prefix numbers) run 12AM–5AM on major routes. Or take a Kakao T taxi — safe and affordable.</p>

  <h2>Subway Etiquette (Important)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Queue at marked positions on the platform</li>
    <li>Let passengers exit before boarding</li>
    <li>Priority seats (pink) for elderly, pregnant, and disabled — always yield</li>
    <li>No eating, no loud phone calls on the train</li>
    <li>Escalators: stand on the right, walk on the left</li>
    <li>Volume down — Koreans are extremely quiet on public transit</li>
  </ul>

